HUDNet: A dynamic calibration method for automotive augmented reality head-up-displays✩
Nan Xu a, Fangzheng Yu a, Jiao Xu b, Dajiang Jin b, Huajun Feng a, Zhihai Xu a, Qi Li a, Yueting Chen a,∗
a College of Optical Science and Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China b Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd, Dongguan, China

ARTICLE INFO
Keywords: Augmented reality Head-up-display Neural networks Calibration Distortion correction

ABSTRACT
The automotive augmented reality head-up-display (AR-HUD) system relies on multiple free-form surfaces to project the virtual image into human eyes. A wide range of views and the extensive display area result in extraordinarily complex distortions. Providing a way to correct such distortions is a major leap forward. Methods widely used for calibration, such as lookup tables and interpolation, require considerable memory capacity, complicated calibration procedures, etc. Additionally, when attempting to meet the high-accuracy requirements for such systems, computational memory grow rapidly. In this context, we propose a fully connected neural network (HUDNet) for an automotive AR-HUD to correct dynamic distortion. Here, we propose a parallel prediction framework for the center and the edge of the image, and separate the distortion information from the disparity information in order to balance the unstable central field of view error. Ultimately, we introduce the transfer learning method to improve accuracy and its effect on accuracy is assessed. The present study provides a reference for us to apply deep learning methods to predict dynamic image distortion of various complex multi-freeform surface reflection systems in the future.

1. Introduction
Modern automobiles are being equipped with ever more innovative technology, as the industry develops rapidly. Augmented reality is a tool to enhance people’s daily life, recreation, and work-related activities. There are several distinctive solutions for automotive augmented reality (AR), including augmented reality head-up display (AR-HUD), which is able to project road tips, signs, and warnings on the windshield in real-time [1]. AR-HUD offers outstanding advantages in terms of improved driving experience and enhanced road safety.
1.1. Challenges and significance of AR-HUD
The idea of using HUD as an Augmented Reality device was originally introduced and explored for avionic applications [2,3]. While an automotive AR-HUD system is an augmented reality display that utilizes the windshield as an optical combiner. The projector system projects the roads’ navigation information or other necessary information on the free-form surface combiner through another pair of free-form surface reflectors, and the driver obtains the fusion information from the virtual image and the world outside. Therefore, drivers

are no longer required to bow their heads and turn their attention to mobile information. They can always be aware of road conditions and respond to all kinds of emergencies more quickly, which increases both safety and comfort.
However, in AR-HUDs, if the user views the virtual image from different perspectives, the image will exhibit varying degrees of distortion and overall translation, as is illustrated in Fig. 1(a). The inaccessibility of the retinal image signifies an additional challenge for the multidirectional dynamic calibration of AR-HUDs [4]. Before it is put into use, the one-to-one correspondence between the human eye position and distortion must be calibrated in a particular manner. During use, the eye-tracking camera obtains eye position [5–7], and the real-time dynamic distortion correction is carried out according to the offline calibration, as shown in Fig. 1(b). Also, the high degree of freedom of the eye position, the complex projector, and the large virtual image size are factors that impede calibration. The complexity and unreliability of the calibration procedures are the major obstacles to the widespread adoption of AR-HUDs in industrial applications. Also, the dynamic alignment between the projected image and the real world (monocular motion disparity) is also one of the issues to be solved. The sum of

