2. Method

The following section presents an overview of the process of acquiring HUD data and the method for estimating the distortion of display elements and explains how our approach overcomes the limitations of existing methods.

2.1. Data collection and processing

A typical projected standard image will produce severe distortion after being reflected by two free-form surface mirrors of the projector and the automobile windshield, as shown in Fig. 1(a). Moreover, the degree and shape of distortion vary depending on the viewpoint from which the projection image is observed. As a result of the difference in relative positions between the human eye and the displayed image as well as the real world, the disparity will also be affected. As long as we recognize the one-to-one correspondence between the pixels in the distorted image and the pixels in the projected image, we can make the distorted image free of distortion by applying a pre-distortion warping to the projected image, as shown in Fig. 1(b). In our case, we are seeking a set of data: 5D input contains the coordinates of the eye in the world coordinate system and a pixel position of the distorted image, while 4D output contains the offset of the entire image caused by the disparity in the two-dimensional display area and the pre-distortion parameters of a pixel of the projected image corresponding to the input. Their relationship is as follows:

