A few works [12,23] assume that the position of the virtual image plane in world coordinates is unchanged, disregarding the disparity problem. Several works [17] treat disparity as a part of generalized distortion, resolving both problems simultaneously. In the case where the pixel is located in the center of the image, if we add distortion and disparity to predict, it will result in a large pre-distortion percentage which reduces the accuracy of our method. We propose to assume that the central dot of the image has no distortion, predict the pre-distortion data, and then add it with the disparity data caused by eye position movement. Therefore, by decoupling the distortion and disparity, we can further improve the prediction accuracy of our method. Also, it can make 𝛿 and 𝑝𝑚𝑎𝑥 lower to reduce 𝐸2 and 𝐸3, as shown in Eq. (4). Additionally, disparity information provides a better interface for the alignment of the projected image and the real scene process, which is also of vital importance.

