It should be noted that the neural network method has other several additional advantages over the traditional calibration method beyond avoiding the steps of surface fitting and inverse transformation as shown in Section 2.1. With the neural network, if you want to increase the accuracy, you simply need to collect more dense data sets, and the amount of storage remains unchanged, which eliminates the problem that the amount of storage in traditional methods increases rapidly as accuracy increases. Moreover, there is a more practical advantage. In the case of identically designed products, an improper installation of the windshield will lead to the problem that the two systems cannot share the same calibration data. It is necessary to collect the same amount of data for each product in order to achieve the same level of accuracy. Within an appropriate modification range, features learned by the neural network can be transmitted [28]. That means the trained network of a product can be used as a pre-training model for another product with installation errors. Therefore, the training can achieve a higher degree of accuracy than when there is no pre-training model as shown in Section 3.3. In other words, the same level of accuracy can be achieved by collecting a smaller amount of data.

2.3. Postprocessing

By running the neural network for an eye position, we can get the pre-distortion parameters of some pixels evenly distributed on the original image (Fig. 4(a)), and then by interpolating, we can calculate the pre-distortion parameters of all pixels(Fig. 4(b)). The projected image input to the AR-HUD system (Fig. 4(c)) can be obtained using the pre-distortion map to pre-distort the original image.

