3.3. Ablation studies
We propose a series of new modules for our calibration method. We will begin with Li’s model as 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 1 for ablation experiments. Gradually adding the new method we proposed to compare the accuracy improvement brought about by each step. As a first step, we add a three-way input block as shown in Eq. (6) to the model (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 2). Then we introduce res-block to deepen and widen the network model (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 3), improve the parameters, and enhance fitting capability. We incrementally add position coding modules (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 4) to the network. Finally, we add percentage parallel prediction (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 5) and coordinate offset prediction (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 6) separately (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 5, 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 6) and simultaneously (𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 7) to verify the importance of data preprocessing. As a result, we present the RSME and maximum error of 𝑢 and 𝑣.
Table 4 illustrates that although each step contributed to the improvement of accuracy, the most significant step was the introduction of offset prediction (Model 5–7). Additionally, the increase in network parameters (Model 3) and prior information (Model 2) contributes to reducing the prediction error also. When we use position encoding (Model 4), the actual coordinate prediction becomes worse because of overfitting. But according to our experiment, for the prediction of coordinate offset, it is useful due to the high complexity.
The deeper and wider network model will reduce the running speed and reduce the frame rate. We find that a reduction in the model depth reduces the network capacity less than a reduction in the model width when the amount of computation is insufficient and the amount of network parameters needs to be reduced.

