As shown in Table 5, when keeping the width of the network unchanged and increasing the depth of the network, after reaching a certain depth, the network will have its maximum accuracy. Beyond this depth, the network fitting ability will deteriorate as the gradient disappears. However, as the network depth increases, the prediction accuracy increases very slowly. In cases where the depth of the network is maintained as it is, and the width of the network is gradually expanded, the effect is obviously better. The highest result is achieved when the network width is 1024. Unfortunately, due to the limited amount of calculation, we were unable to choose a width of 1024 and a depth of 8. A network with ten layers is the most effective, and a network with eight layers is selected. Even if the calculation amount must be further reduced, a network with six layers may be selected as the width reduction will lead to even greater reductions in prediction accuracy.
3.4. System analysis for generalization of HUDNet
As a final step, to investigate the generalization of HUDNet for different product calibration data, we use simulation software to simulate the installation error of HUD 𝑆𝑦𝑠𝑡𝑒𝑚 𝐴, as shown in Fig. 7, collect the 9 × 27 dot-matrix data of 5 × 5 × 7 viewpoints, and train the 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 𝐴 as a pre-training model. Then we get a 𝑆𝑦𝑠𝑡𝑒𝑚 𝐵 by rotating the windshield 2 degrees around the Y axis and get a 𝑆𝑦𝑠𝑡𝑒𝑚 𝐶 by moving the windshield 2 mm in the negative direction of the Z axis. For the two new systems, we train 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 𝐵 and 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 𝐶 respectively. After all, we train 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 𝐵𝑃 and 𝑀𝑜𝑑𝑒𝑙 𝐶𝑃 with the pre-training model. Table 6 presents the results.
Our study found that on the same designed system when errors occur during the actual installation, the network prediction accuracy is similar regardless of whether the calibration data is different between different systems. The HUDNet predictions will be much more accurate if a system that experiences installation errors already has a training model for another system as a pre-training model. As you can see, if you want to improve the calibration accuracy in the actual production process, you can obtain as many data points as possible from a system in order to raise the accuracy of the model. Then save the model as a pre-training model for subsequent products. Transfer learning allows the calibration accuracy of subsequent products to be improved. As an alternative, if you want to reduce the cost of manual data collection,

