4. Conclusion
AR-HUD demand is rapidly increasing, and this technology will soon become an essential part of the automotive industry. Current HUDs, however, are far from perfect.
In this paper, we propose a complete calibration process, including data acquisition, data preprocessing, calibration, pre-distortion prediction, and post-processing. This paper applies deep learning to the dynamic distortion calibration of AR-HUD systems. In light of the characteristics of deep learning, we recommend that reprocessing the obtained data prior to inputting it into the neural network for training will be more accurate than directly inputting the obtained data. There has been considerable improvement in the performance of the neural network, regardless of distortion percentage or position encoding. Additionally, for the task of distortion calibration, we investigated the influence of the network structure on the calibration results and proposed HUDNet as a candidate. HUDNet can improve the accuracy not only by increasing the training data without increasing the amount of running computation memory but also by avoiding the process of surface fitting and interpolation. Most importantly, we further explore the generalizability of neural network models for different products. We have demonstrated that the accuracy of distortion correction of real products can be improved through transfer learning, further reducing the cost of manual data collection.
The process of the system is that the monitoring camera monitors the human eye position data, and the neural network provides distortion prediction results according to the data. Therefore, the potential for erroneous operations is determined by the stability of the algorithm. Theoretically, the error rate of the algorithm will be far less than the error rate of manual operation and debugging. Moreover, since the system has no cumulative error in the timeline, the dynamic distortion correction refresh frame rate is 30 Hz, and each system will be recalibrated, transfer learning and testing, the occasional error will not have a great impact.
Even though the use of generalization reduces the collection of data sets across different types of vehicles, it is still necessary to collect data manually. The next topic may be how to predict the relationship between installation tolerance and distortion using only one manual calibration. A predictive way of adapting to every installation tolerance further may support industrial mass production of AR-HUD systems. Also, the criticality of HUD errors is biased. Error tolerances in the HUD center (<5 mrad) are much smaller than the outer edges. In subsequent work, a more accurate error function is needed to better fit this bias. There also needs further research on how to match the camera optical system with the human eye optical system by calibrating camera lens distortion parameters.
